    "reviews": [
      {
        "author_name": "Gerald Boisvert",
        "author_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/contrib/115078862172418441936/reviews",
        "language": "en",
        "original_language": "en",
        "profile_photo_url": "https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocIZkFQkOQPRWxxGiIvAw4Irh_rmzLotMQMTGV5bpIMpC-XJFA=s128-c0x00000000-cc-rp-mo-ba2",
        "rating": 1,
        "relative_time_description": "7 months ago",
        "text": "I would give them a 0 if I could.\n\nAfter having an appointment to get work done, then many calls and reminders later, after 6 months of waiting, I called and gave them 1 month to get the job done. They didn't get the job even started. At that point they got a new man on the job of planning the work, etc. It was much better, and he promised to get the work done in a week (or less, I forget which). The electrician came on time on the day promised and did a sloppy job. When he was finished, he left. I inspected the job done and was dissatisfied with the work. The electrician was called back, and I was even less satisfied. He couldn't even line up the breakers so that they were all straight and we no longer had to force the cover in place, and it lessened the gaps that were dangerously wide. I sent him away and straightened the breakers myself. Apparently, that by itself was enough to void the warranty on the job and the equipment.\n\nAfter about a month, we had power dimmed out. It turned out that one of the two 110 volt lines was giving only about 60 volts on the average. The other was giving full voltage. I asked a friend, a licensed, qualified electrician who works for another company a few questions, and he decided to inspect the work. I also inspected the work. We both came to the same conclusion that the problem was between the meter and the connection at the top of the mast. The box containing the meter, along with the connections to Hydro Quebec source had been changed by Systeme Electriques D'arc, and the work was defective.\n\nI was able to get an authorization number from Hydro Quebec, and contacted Systemes Electriques to correct the work. They were only interested in sending me back to Hydro Quebec to do the work, even though I already had an authorization for any qualified electrician to do the job. Rather than doing to job, they spent their time telling me that the work and equipment was no longer under warranty because I had inspected and moved a few of the breakers so that we would have a working fridge and freezer. We had been over a day without them working. We came very close to losing hundreds of dollars of food. The house was very hot because anything that ran on 220 volts was not functional, including the thermo-pump.\n\nSo, I got somebody else to do the work.",
        "time": 1752516398,
        "translated": false
      },
      {
        "author_name": "Roman Kotchetkov",
        "author_url": "https://www.google.com/maps/contrib/102451263395520589788/reviews",
